---
title: "Using layaR with the tidyverse"
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
# Precomputed: knitted locally by data-raw/knit_vignettes.R.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.path = "tidyverse-",
                      fig.width = 7, fig.height = 3.5, dpi = 120)
options(width = 90, pillar.print_min = 10)
```

layaR has no tidyverse dependency, but its outputs are ordinary data
frames, factors and lists, so they fit into a dplyr pipeline without any
glue. This article repeats the workflows of
`vignette("data-frames", package = "layaR")` in tidyverse style: annotate a
tibble in a pipe, summarise the answers, unpack the probability
list-columns, and turn batch results into tidy tables.

```{r, message = FALSE}
library(layaR)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(purrr)
library(ggplot2)
```

```{r}
questions <- laya_questions(
  department = laya_choice(
    "Which department should handle this request?",
    billing   = "invoices, payments, refunds",
    technical = "bugs, outages, system errors",
    sales     = "pricing, new contracts",
    other     = "everything else"
  ),
  urgency = laya_score("How urgent is this request?",
                       c("not urgent", "soon", "critical deadline or blocking issue")),
  churn_risk = laya_noul("Does the customer say they will cancel or leave?")
)

tickets <- tibble(
  id = 1:6,
  subject = c("Duplicate charge", "App crashes on login", "Pricing for 50 seats",
              "Cancel my account", "Export to CSV?", "Server down!!!"),
  body = c(
    "We were billed twice for March. Refund it or we cancel.",
    "Since the last update the app closes as soon as I log in.",
    "We're a team of 50 and want a quote for the annual plan.",
    "Your service has been unreliable for months. Please close my account.",
    "Is there a way to export my reports to CSV?",
    "Production is down and our customers can't check out. Fix this now."
  )
)
```

## Annotating in a pipe

`laya_annotate()` takes the data first, so it drops straight into a pipe. A
tibble goes in and a tibble comes out, with the answers added as columns:

```{r}
annotated <- tickets |>
  laya_annotate(questions, state = c("subject", "body"))

annotated |> select(-subject, -body)
```

`state` takes the column names as strings (`c("subject", "body")`), not bare
names: write `state = c("subject", "body")`, not `state = c(subject, body)`.

A choice question gives a factor whose levels are the options in order, so
`count()` keeps them in that order. Add `.drop = FALSE` to show options no
row chose:

```{r}
annotated |> count(department, .drop = FALSE)
```

A yes/no answer is a probability, so filtering on it is a threshold:

```{r}
annotated |>
  filter(churn_risk > 0.5) |>
  arrange(desc(churn_risk)) |>
  select(id, subject, churn_risk)
```

And the usual grouped summaries work:

```{r}
annotated |>
  group_by(department) |>
  summarise(tickets = n(),
            mean_urgency = mean(urgency),
            at_risk = sum(churn_risk > 0.5))
```

## Unpacking the probabilities

`probabilities = TRUE` adds an `<id>_probabilities` list-column per
question, each element a named numeric vector. `tidyr::unnest_wider()`
spreads one into a column per option:

```{r}
with_probs <- tickets |>
  laya_annotate(questions, state = c("subject", "body"), probabilities = TRUE)

with_probs |>
  select(id, department_probabilities) |>
  unnest_wider(department_probabilities)
```

A score question has one column per level, and a yes/no question has
`false` and `true`:

```{r}
with_probs |>
  select(id, urgency_probabilities) |>
  unnest_wider(urgency_probabilities)
```

`unnest_longer()` gives one row per option instead. From there, the
probability of the option each row chose is a filter. That is the number
`min_confidence` compares with the threshold:

```{r}
gated <- tickets |>
  laya_annotate(questions["department"], state = c("subject", "body"),
                min_confidence = 0.99, probabilities = TRUE)

gated |>
  select(id, department, department_abstained, department_probabilities) |>
  unnest_longer(department_probabilities, indices_to = "option",
                values_to = "chosen_p") |>
  filter(option == department) |>
  select(id, department, chosen_p, department_abstained)
```

The answer is kept even when the model abstains, so you choose what to do
with uncertain rows, for example send them to a person:

```{r}
gated |>
  mutate(route = if_else(department_abstained, "human review", as.character(department))) |>
  count(route)
```

## Batch results as tidy tables

`laya_predict_batch()` returns a list of full results, one per state.
`as.data.frame()` turns it into a long table with one row per state and
question, and `laya_probabilities()` gives one row per option. Both are
plain data frames; `as_tibble()` makes them tibbles.

```{r}
batch <- laya_predict_batch(tickets |> select(subject, body), questions, progress = FALSE)

long <- as_tibble(as.data.frame(batch))
long
```

`answer` is text, so answers of every type fit in one column; `value` is
the number behind it (for a choice question, the chosen option's
probability). `pivot_wider()` turns the long table into one row per state:

```{r}
long |>
  select(state_id, id, answer) |>
  pivot_wider(names_from = id, values_from = answer)
```

The per-option table is the right shape for ggplot2:

```{r department-probabilities}
laya_probabilities(batch) |>
  filter(id == "department") |>
  mutate(option = factor(option, levels = names(questions$department$criteria)),
         ticket = factor(tickets$subject[state_id], levels = rev(tickets$subject))) |>
  ggplot(aes(probability, ticket, fill = option)) +
  geom_col(position = position_stack(reverse = TRUE)) +
  scale_x_continuous(labels = scales::percent) +
  labs(x = "Probability", y = NULL, fill = "Department") +
  theme_minimal()
```

## Working with result lists using purrr

Each result is a list, so purrr's typed maps pull out single values. This
is the tidyverse version of the `sapply()` / `vapply()` code in the other
vignettes:

```{r}
batch |> map_dbl(\(r) r$answers$churn_risk$noul)
batch |> map_chr(\(r) r$answers$department$choice)
```

A good use is comparing question wordings against a few labelled examples,
as `vignette("writing-questions", package = "layaR")` recommends:

```{r}
examples <- tibble(
  text = c(
    "Duplicate charge. We were billed twice for March. Refund it or we cancel.",
    "Your service has been unreliable for months. Please close my account.",
    "Please refund the duplicate today or we will cancel our plan.",
    "Since the last update the app closes as soon as I log in.",
    "We're a team of 50 and want a quote for the annual plan."
  ),
  cancels = c(TRUE, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE)
)
wordings <- laya_questions(
  threaten = laya_noul("Does the user threaten to cancel or leave?"),
  say      = laya_noul("Does the customer say they will cancel or leave?"),
  mention  = laya_noul("Does the customer mention cancelling their plan, subscription or account?")
)

scored <- examples |>
  laya_annotate(wordings, state = "text", confidence = FALSE) |>
  pivot_longer(c(threaten, say, mention), names_to = "wording", values_to = "p")

scored |>
  group_by(wording) |>
  summarise(correct = sum((p > 0.5) == cancels),
            min_p_when_true = min(p[cancels]),
            max_p_when_false = max(p[!cancels]))
```

`laya_annotate()` with one question per wording does the same job as the
`sapply()` loop, and the long table makes it easy to score each wording.
